<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Kilkenny</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> is a county in the south-east of Ireland, in the province of Leinster, centred on the city of Kilkenny on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-nore">River Nore</a>. It corresponds broadly to the early medieval kingdom of Osraige. After the Anglo-Norman conquest it was part of the Marshal lordship of Leinster and later a stronghold of the Butler earls of Ormond, and in the 1640s Kilkenny was the seat of the Irish Confederate government.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> is the 16th-largest of Ireland's 32 counties by area and borders five others: Tipperary to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> to the south, Carlow and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a> to the east and Laois to the north. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-nore">River Nore</a> flows through the county, and the River Suir forms the border with Waterford. Most of the county is hilly, with uplands in the north-east, north-west and south, and its highest point is Brandon Hill at 515 metres.</p><p>Most of the rock is limestone. A large area in the north and east, around Castlecomer, holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> beds, generally with culm, which was used extensively for burning lime. Near Ballyhale the Kiltorcan Formation, at the top of the Old Red Sandstone, contains a fossil assemblage that includes Archaeopteris.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county takes its name from the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a>, the anglicised form of Cill Chainnigh, the church of Cainneach or Canice. This probably refers to the church and round tower that became St Canice's Cathedral. The county was based on the Gaelic Kingdom of Osraige, or Ossory, which existed from at least the 2nd century until the 13th century. Its kings, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-giolla-phadraig">Mac Giolla Phádraig</a> family, ruled from a stronghold at Cill Chainnigh. The medieval Diocese of Ossory was established in 549 and its territory matched the kingdom's. In historic times Kilkenny replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe</a> as the chief church of Osraige.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> was the last county in Leinster, apart from Louth, to have native speakers of Irish. Its dialect, part of the Ossory dialect region shared with Laois, had most of its features in common with the surviving dialect of Ring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-waterford">County Waterford</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county had 103,685 people at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> on the Nore is its largest settlement and seat of local government. Its buildings include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny-castle">Kilkenny Castle</a>, Rothe House, St Canice's Cathedral and St Mary's Cathedral in the city, Jerpoint Abbey near Thomastown and Kells Priory. Dunmore Cave at Ballyfoyle is one of the most documented archaeological sites in Ireland. In 1966 the county's village creameries amalgamated to form Avonmore Creameries. Hurling is by far the dominant sport, and the county's colours are black and amber, first worn by Tullaroan in 1886.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Kilkenny” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Coriondi appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, placed in the interior of south-eastern Ireland, roughly in modern Counties <b>Kilkenny</b>, Carlow or Wexford, near the Brigantes (Ireland) and the Usdiae.</blockquote><a href="/state/coriondi">Coriondi</a></li><li><blockquote>Osraige emerged in early medieval Ireland as the kingdom of a people living in the valleys of the Nore and its tributaries, in modern <b>County Kilkenny</b> and south Laois.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-osraige">Kingdom of Osraige</a></li></ul></section>
